<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#federalism">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Federalism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as federalisation, federal autonomy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Federalism is a system of government in which power is divided between a central authority and constituent units. Proposals to federalise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> were discussed as a way to satisfy its many nationalities but were never carried out. The term is also used to describe the substantial local autonomy that cities and provinces enjoyed within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/federalism.jpg" alt="Federalism" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_esp_Rep._Federal,_Rep._Unitaria_(2).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The words federalism and confederalism share a root in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> foedus, meaning treaty, pact or covenant. Until the late eighteenth century they meant much the same thing, a simple league among sovereign states based on a treaty. The first forms of federalism were alliances between tribes or city states in ancient times, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a>. The Achaean League of Hellenistic Greece is considered an early ancestor, since cities then banded together even at the cost of surrendering part of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Johannes Althusius is considered the father of modern federalism, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, and in the modern era it was first adopted by a union of states of the Old Swiss Confederacy from the mid-14th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Federalism combines a general level of government with a regional level of sub-unit governments, such as states, provinces or cantons, and divides the powers of governing between them. It differs from confederalism, where the central government is subordinate to the regional states, and from the unitary state, where the regional level is subordinate to the centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> called the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a> neither a national nor a federal constitution, but a composition of both, and in the nineteenth century federalism in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> came to mean this new compound form alone.</p><p>In a federation the division of power is usually set out in the constitution, and the right of the component states to self-government is entrenched. Foreign policy and defence are almost always federal powers. An upper house often represents the component states, as in the Senate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and the German Bundesrat. Constitutional amendment often requires the consent of the states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Proponents have argued that the checks and the sharing of power in a federation reduce threats from within and abroad and allow a state to be both large and diverse. The word has opposite senses on two continents. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the Federalists of the founding era wanted a stronger central government, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> federalists favour a common government above the nations, an idea that grew out of post-war Europe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>'s speech in Zürich in 1946. Anarchists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</a> envisaged a stateless federalism built from the bottom up.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Federalism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Attempts at <b>federalisation</b> or broader national rights were consistently blocked by German-speaking parties in Austria or by the Hungarian political elite, who feared any arrangement that might dilute their privileged position within the monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The programme provided for the <b>federalisation</b> of the country into two equal nations, and its foreign policy combined good relations with Western countries and cooperation with the Soviet Union and the rest of the Eastern Bloc.</blockquote><a href="/movement/prague-spring">Prague Spring · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
